<quiz><question type="stack">
    <name>
      <text>Probability 1.2 (copy)</text>
    </name>
    <questiontext format="html">
      <text><![CDATA[<p>There are {@prob_total@} students in a certain tutor group at Philips College.  There are {@prob_german@} students in the tutor group studying German, {@prob_french@} studying French and {@prob_both@} students studying both French and German. Find the probability that a randomly chosen student in the tutor group.</p>
<p>a) Studies French: [[input:ans1]] [[validation:ans1]] [[feedback:prt1]]</p>
<p>b) Studies French and German: [[input:ans2]] [[validation:ans2]]  [[feedback:prt2]]</p> 
<p>c) Studies French but not German: [[input:ans3]] [[validation:ans3]]  [[feedback:prt3]]</p>
<p>d) Does not study French or German: [[input:ans4]] [[validation:ans4]]  [[feedback:prt4]]</p>]]></text>
    </questiontext>
    <generalfeedback format="html">
      <text><![CDATA[<p><b>Worked solution</b></p>
<p>We recall that if \(G\) is the event "The chosen student studies German" then the probability of \(G\) is given by the formula \(P(G) = \frac{\mbox{card } (G)}{\mbox{Total number of students}} \).</p>
<p>For instance, here card(G)\(={@prob_german@}\) and Total number of students \(={@prob_total@}\)</p>
<hr>
<p>a) Find the probability that a randomly chosen student in the tutor group studies French.</p>
<p>Let the event \(F\) : "The chosen student studies French". Then</p>
<p>\(P(F) =\frac{\mbox{card}(F)}{@prob_total@}\) where card(\(F\)) represents the number of students studying French. So&nbsp; card(\(F\))\(= {@prob_french@} \) </p>
<p> Therefore, \( P(F)= {@ta1@} \)</p>
<hr>
<p>b) Find the probability that a randomly chosen student in the tutor group studies French and German.</p> 
<p>Let the event \(F \cap G\) : "The chosen student studies French and German". Then</p>
\(P(F \cap G) =\frac{\mbox{card}( F&nbsp; \cap &nbsp;G)}{@prob_total@}\)  where card(\(F \cap G\)) represents the number of students studying French and German. So&nbsp; card(\(F \cap G\))\(={@prob_both@} \) <p></p>
<p>Therefore, \( P(F \cap G)= {@ta2@} \)</p>
<hr>
<p>c) Find the probability that a randomly chosen student in the tutor group studies French but not German.</p>
<p> Let \(C\) be the event: "The chosen student studies French but not German". </p>
<p> \(P(C) =\frac{\mbox{card}(C)}{@prob_total@}\) </p><p>
</p><p>But card(\(C\))\(=\)card(\(F\)) \(-\) card(\(F \cap G\)) \(= {@prob_french-prob_both@} \)</p>
<p>Therefore, \( P(C)=  {@ta3@}\) </p>
<hr>
<p>d) Find the probability that a randomly chosen student in the tutor group does not study French or German. </p>
<p> Let \(D\) be the event: "The chosen student does not study French or German". </p>
<p> \(P(D) =\frac{\mbox{card}(D)}{@prob_total@}\) </p><p>
</p><p>We recall that the language "does not study French or German" means "does not study French and does not study German".</p>
<p>First, we need to find the number of students studying German but not French, which we denote by card(\(G'\)). Then</p>
<p>card(\(G'\))\(= \) card (G) \(-\) card(\(F \cap G\))\(={@card_onlygerman@}\)</p>
Thus,
<p>card(\(D\)) \(={@prob_total@} - \) (card(\(C\)) \(+\) card(\(G'\)) \(+\) card(\(F \cap G\)) )\(= {@prob_notgerfre@}\)</p>
<p>Therefore, \(P(D)={@ta4@}\)</p>]]></text>
    </generalfeedback>
